WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.517
[MUSIC]

00:00:08.517 --> 00:00:11.834
There's a lot of kind of discussion
of what the political beliefs of

00:00:11.834 --> 00:00:15.790
Silicon Valley elites and founders are,
but we actually have no hard data on what

00:00:15.790 --> 00:00:19.230
they actually believe when it
comes to politics and policy.

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:23.045
&gt;&gt; Some people say Silicon Valley is
really libertarian, other people say,

00:00:23.045 --> 00:00:27.530
Silicon Valley, they're all just like
loyal democrats, Silicon Valley, they all

00:00:27.530 --> 00:00:31.450
just care about the social issues and
they're very far left on social issues.

00:00:31.450 --> 00:00:33.520
However, although there's no
a lot of thinking about, for

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:36.130
example, people on Wall Street,
how do they think about politics?

00:00:36.130 --> 00:00:37.160
What do they want?

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:39.090
How do they interface with
the political system?

00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:42.936
There'd be much less thinking about
that same question on the context of

00:00:42.936 --> 00:00:43.888
Silicon Valley.

00:00:43.888 --> 00:00:49.246
[MUSIC]

00:00:49.246 --> 00:00:53.841
&gt;&gt; Technology elites are basically liberal
on every dimension you could think of

00:00:53.841 --> 00:00:55.780
except regulation.

00:00:55.780 --> 00:01:00.720
And this is actually really interesting
because there is no other group

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:05.810
of people that we surveyed, that have
this constellation of political beliefs.

00:01:05.810 --> 00:01:10.611
And specifically when you come to economic
issues, they are not easily categorized as

00:01:10.611 --> 00:01:14.821
libertarians, because they believe
in the redistribution of income, for

00:01:14.821 --> 00:01:18.329
things like health care and
education and poverty reduction.

00:01:18.329 --> 00:01:19.417
But at the same time,

00:01:19.417 --> 00:01:23.130
they believe that government
should not be regulating business.

00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:29.040
And some people have now called this new
type of political animal, the libertarian.

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:32.281
So they're trying to find new words and
terminologies, and so

00:01:32.281 --> 00:01:36.502
it's been kind of interesting to see our
people try to Introducing new categories

00:01:36.502 --> 00:01:38.725
to match the results we've been finding.

00:01:38.725 --> 00:01:44.946
[MUSIC]

00:01:44.946 --> 00:01:48.290
&gt;&gt; A lot of Silicon Valley companies right
now are innovating and labor markets.

00:01:48.290 --> 00:01:53.055
Uber is a great example, where there's
a question of whether gig workers for

00:01:53.055 --> 00:01:57.055
Uber should be treated like
regular workers or as contractors.

00:01:57.055 --> 00:02:00.025
You can imagine labor unions see
that as a classic case where

00:02:00.025 --> 00:02:02.375
it's important these workers
receive protections,

00:02:02.375 --> 00:02:05.470
all the protections that would be
associated with traditional employees.

00:02:05.470 --> 00:02:08.526
Whereas technology entrepreneurs
look at this and they say,

00:02:08.526 --> 00:02:11.992
no, we shouldn't be regulating how
these companies treat workers,

00:02:11.992 --> 00:02:14.419
we should give them freedom
in the marketplace.

00:02:14.419 --> 00:02:17.574
And so that's a classic example where I
think in a lot of states that usually

00:02:17.574 --> 00:02:18.775
starting to come do ahead and

00:02:18.775 --> 00:02:22.078
you could imagine that is sort of thing
that those who were to protect companies

00:02:22.078 --> 00:02:24.981
who were doing more donating in
democratic campaigns are going to say,

00:02:24.981 --> 00:02:27.880
actually we disagree with the labor
unions that are endorsing you.

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:30.330
We think you should take this
other position on this issue.

00:02:30.330 --> 00:02:33.029
So those kinds of tensions
within the democratic party,

00:02:33.029 --> 00:02:35.403
we think they going to
become increasingly common.

00:02:35.403 --> 00:02:41.701
[MUSIC]

00:02:41.701 --> 00:02:45.904
&gt;&gt; A lot of historical research has found
that parties usually change when there's

00:02:45.904 --> 00:02:50.044
important groups within those parties
that agree with them on most things, but

00:02:50.044 --> 00:02:52.081
disagree with them on a few things, and

00:02:52.081 --> 00:02:54.960
then they shape the party
in that direction.

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:58.080
So a classic example is that the AFL-CIO,

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:03.580
the CIO portion of it before it merged,
was very tolerant of racial progress.

00:03:03.580 --> 00:03:04.660
And at the time,

00:03:04.660 --> 00:03:09.630
the democrats had a lot of virulent
racist people as leaders in that party,

00:03:09.630 --> 00:03:14.270
and they sort of used labor, which they
agreed with the Democratic Party on, and

00:03:14.270 --> 00:03:18.120
shifted the Democratic Party to being
more of a ProCivil rights party.

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:21.390
And this pattern has emerged over and
over again in American history.

00:03:21.390 --> 00:03:26.300
So one prediction our research suggests
is that, as technology leads become more

00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:30.690
important part of the Democratic Party,
in terms of its fund raising apparatus,

00:03:30.690 --> 00:03:35.080
they're going to double down and promote
traditional democratic interests when it

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:40.105
comes to social policy, redistribution,
foreign policy, immigration.

00:03:40.105 --> 00:03:45.155
But try to move it towards the right
on issues when it comes to regulation,

00:03:45.155 --> 00:03:47.446
particularly of labor markets.

00:03:47.446 --> 00:03:52.578
[MUSIC]

00:03:52.578 --> 00:03:57.944
I think a big question right now in
politics is the gap between elites and

00:03:57.944 --> 00:03:59.160
masses.

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:04.210
And for the whole post World War period,
there's been sort of an agreement

00:04:04.210 --> 00:04:08.120
that we would devolve some power to
elites who would then look out for

00:04:08.120 --> 00:04:09.880
like everybody's interest.

00:04:09.880 --> 00:04:15.014
But increasingly we've seen populist
revolts going on in the United States and

00:04:15.014 --> 00:04:16.935
all the way across the world.

00:04:16.935 --> 00:04:21.012
What people and the masses have kind
of realized are wages have stagnated,

00:04:21.012 --> 00:04:24.830
expenses in real terms have gone up
on things like health insurance and

00:04:24.830 --> 00:04:28.972
college tuition etcetera, and our children
are going to be living worse lives

00:04:28.972 --> 00:04:31.980
with a worse standard
of living than we did.

00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:37.965
And so there's a lot of discussion about
well, how does our democratic institutions

00:04:37.965 --> 00:04:43.090
going to survive if there's not going to
be cooperation between masses and elites?

00:04:43.090 --> 00:04:47.140
So I think a lot of this research has to
continue to explore this question more.

00:04:47.140 --> 00:04:50.770
I think Dave and I are just generally
interested in kind of understanding, well,

00:04:50.770 --> 00:04:53.790
who are the elites and what does
that mean and what do they believe?

00:04:53.790 --> 00:04:57.820
And before we did a study like this,
there really was no data, and

00:04:57.820 --> 00:05:02.020
it's because these are very hard people
to interview and get their opinions on.

00:05:02.020 --> 00:05:04.860
And I think just future work
needs to be done in this topic so

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:06.820
we can understand the contours and

00:05:06.820 --> 00:05:10.880
potential fault lines in politics,
both in the United States and abroad.

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:15.028
&gt;&gt; The other transformation you've seen
economically for a lot of Americans is not

00:05:15.028 --> 00:05:18.817
only have we just stagnated, but
people are exposed to increasing risk.

00:05:18.817 --> 00:05:23.653
And so a lot of the kind of government run
programs that technology entrepreneurs can

00:05:23.653 --> 00:05:24.958
be more skeptical of.

00:05:24.958 --> 00:05:26.281
They're not just about
transferring resources,

00:05:26.281 --> 00:05:27.880
they're about some form
of social insurance.

00:05:27.880 --> 00:05:30.340
And so I think as people
are exposed to more and more risk,

00:05:30.340 --> 00:05:33.940
that might be an experience that
technology entrepreneurs haven't directly

00:05:33.940 --> 00:05:37.990
experienced as much, and so that's another
gap that I'm interested in looking at.

00:05:37.990 --> 00:05:42.020
&gt;&gt; I'm a big believer that I think social
science should be addressing questions

00:05:42.020 --> 00:05:44.100
that are important to the broader world.

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:47.650
That if the world says,
these are the issues that are important

00:05:47.650 --> 00:05:51.200
to how human beings are going to have
to grapple with their democracies,

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:55.040
their societies, then that's what
academics should be studying.

00:05:55.040 --> 00:05:59.622
So that's one reason I was very proud
of this study, is that these issues of

00:05:59.622 --> 00:06:04.862
inequality, automation, technology, this
is what the world is facing right now,

00:06:04.862 --> 00:06:09.274
and so I think it's important to
address questions that are relevant.

00:06:09.274 --> 00:06:17.400
[MUSIC]

